(define (problem site-survey)

  ;; Three robots at the depot. `scout` and `relay` are the survey team;
  ;; `observer` is not, and the mission is written so that the team cannot
  ;; simply shout.
  ;;
  ;; As in any honest sensing problem, the initial state does not say whether
  ;; the site is contaminated. The theory leaves it open, so the model
  ;; designates two worlds and the solution has to branch.

  (:domain survey-team)

  (:requirements
    :equality :list-comprehensions
    :finitary-S5-theories :modal-goals :knowing-whether
    :multi-pointed-models :negative-preconditions
  )

  (:agents scout relay observer)

  (:init
    (:and
      (:forall (?i - agent)
        ([C. All] (not (on-site ?i))))
      (:forall (?i - agent)
        ([C. All] (<Kw. ?i> (contaminated))))
    )
  )

  ;; Three conjuncts, and each one rules out a different shortcut.
  ;;
  ;;   scout must know whether    -- somebody has to go and look
  ;;   relay must know whether    -- the finding has to travel. Note that this
  ;;                                 is knowledge of the finding itself, not
  ;;                                 knowledge *about scout's* knowledge: the
  ;;                                 weaker, second-order goal
  ;;                                 ([relay] ([Kw. scout] (contaminated)))
  ;;                                 is already satisfied by the scan, because
  ;;                                 semi-private sensing lets the others see
  ;;                                 that a scan happened. Asking for it would
  ;;                                 be asking for something the mission gets
  ;;                                 for nothing
  ;;   observer must not know     -- and so the finding cannot travel over the
  ;;     whether                     open channel
  ;;
  ;; The three together admit only one shape of solution: a team member goes,
  ;; scans, and tells the other over a link the observer cannot hear.
  ;;
  ;; Measured, by removing conjuncts and re-planning:
  ;;
  ;;   without the third   the planner sends `observer` itself to scan and
  ;;                       then broadcast. Three actions, and 32 expansions
  ;;   with all three      a team member scans and uses the private link.
  ;;                       Three actions, and 40 expansions
  ;;
  ;; Both plans are the same length, which is worth noting because it is the
  ;; opposite of what one expects: secrecy does not cost the mission an extra
  ;; step. What it does is make a whole class of solutions inadmissible, and
  ;; the search work goes up rather than the plan.
  ;;
  ;; Weakening the second conjunct to the second-order
  ;; ([relay] ([Kw. scout] (contaminated))) collapses the mission to two
  ;; actions, because semi-private sensing already tells the others that a scan
  ;; happened.
  (:goal
    (and
      ([Kw. scout] (contaminated))
      ([Kw. relay] (contaminated))
      (<Kw. observer> (contaminated))
    )
  )
)
